{"qid":"Q2984183","names":["Duchies of Poland"],"title":"Duchies of Poland","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Duchies of Poland (1138–c. 1305) refers to the period of Polish political fragmentation that began when Bolesław III Wrymouth's testament, enacted at his death in 1138, divided the realm among his sons under a senioral principle that broke down almost immediately: Władysław II was expelled in 1146 and his Silesian line eventually became a separate Piast dynasty under Bohemian suzerainty. The Mongol invasion of 1241, which devastated Kraków and Legnica, accelerated demographic and political disruption. Gradual reunification was pursued by successive Piast dukes, culminating in the coronation of Władysław I Łokietek (the Elbow-high) as King of Poland at Kraków in 1320, though Silesia and Pomerelia had by then been severed.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The changing inter-ducal borders are among the least precisely documented of medieval European frontiers; the polygon representing this entity necessarily generalizes across the overlapping jurisdictions of multiple Piast branches.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Poland — The Piast dynasty","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Poland/The-Piast-dynasty"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Testament of Bolesław III Wrymouth","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Testament_of_Boles%C5%82aw_III_Wrymouth"},{"label":"Piotr Górecki, Economy, Society and Lordship in Medieval Poland 1100–1250 (Holmes & Meier, 1992)","url":"https://archive.org/details/economysocietyl00gore"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Testament of Bolesław III Wrymouth"